            <ArticleTitle>No evidence of increased prevalence of premature coronary artery disease in New York City police officers as predicted by coronary artery calcium scoring.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>661-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/JOM.0b013e3181e36457</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the prevalence of coronary artery disease (CAD) in active New York City police officers as detected by coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We assessed 2064 New York City police officers who underwent electron beam computed tomography for quantification of CAC.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The mean age of study subjects was 42 +/- 6 years. A CAC score of 0 was present in 74% of men and 80% of women. A subset of 75 officers with known early exposure to World Trade Center dust were evaluated separately.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">New York City police officers do not have an increased prevalence of CAD compared with the general population as assessed with CAC scoring. At 5 years, exposure to World Trade Center dust does not appear to increase the risk of premature CAD.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
